{
 'title': 'From the Grand Tour to the package holiday', 'context': 'A lecture in a tourism history course on how travel for pleasure developed in Europe.',
 'vocab': ['Grand Tour', 'aristocrat', 'antiquities', 'excavation', 'package tour'],
 'script': [
  ('Lecturer', 'AU', "Good afternoon. In this lecture, I want to look at one of the most important chapters in the history of travel: the Grand Tour, and how it eventually gave way to something we would recognise today as modern tourism."),
  ('Lecturer', 'AU', "For most of history, people travelled because they had to. Merchants travelled to trade, soldiers to fight, and pilgrims to visit holy places. The idea of travelling mainly for education and pleasure is much more recent. From around the middle of the seventeenth century until the early nineteenth century, it became fashionable for wealthy young men, particularly from Britain, to spend a long period travelling around continental Europe. This was known as the Grand Tour. The term was popularised by an English priest, Richard Lassels, in a guide to Italy published in 1670."),
  ('Lecturer', 'AU', "The typical Grand Tourist was a young man from an aristocratic family, often in his early twenties, who had just finished his studies at Oxford or Cambridge. He would usually be accompanied by a tutor, whose job was to supervise his education and, ideally, his behaviour. These tutors were sometimes jokingly called 'bear-leaders', because they were said to lead their wild young students around like performing bears. The tour could last anything from a few months to several years."),
  ('Lecturer', 'AU', "The main purpose was education. Young men were expected to improve their knowledge of languages, especially French and Italian, to study art and architecture, and to learn the manners of polite society. The most important destination was Italy, and above all Rome, where travellers could see the remains of the classical world they had read about at school in Latin and Greek texts. Florence, Venice and Naples were also essential stops."),
  ('Lecturer', 'AU', "Getting there was far from easy. Travelling through France meant long days in horse-drawn carriages on poor roads. To reach Italy, many travellers crossed the Alps by the Mont Cenis pass. Carriages could not be driven over the pass, so they were taken apart and carried by mules, while the travellers themselves were carried up and down the mountain in chairs by local porters. Many wrote home describing how terrified they had been."),
  ('Lecturer', 'AU', "Grand Tourists were also enthusiastic collectors. They brought home paintings, sculptures, books and antiquities, many of which can still be seen in the great country houses of Britain. Views of Venice by the painter Canaletto were especially popular, and in Rome many visitors had their portraits painted by Pompeo Batoni, often posing in front of famous ancient ruins. The excavations at Herculaneum and Pompeii, which began in the 1730s and 1740s, added a new attraction, as travellers could watch buried Roman towns being uncovered."),
  ('Lecturer', 'AU', "During the eighteenth century, the Grand Tour gradually became less exclusive, and growing numbers of middle-class travellers and women took part. But it was badly interrupted by the French Revolution and the wars that followed, which made travel across much of Europe dangerous until 1815."),
  ('Lecturer', 'AU', "The real transformation came with the railways. In 1841, Thomas Cook, a Baptist preacher who campaigned against alcohol, organised a special train to take around five hundred people from Leicester to a temperance meeting in Loughborough. The ticket cost one shilling, and the price included food. This trip is often described as the beginning of the package tour, because one person arranged the transport, the meals and the activities for a whole group. Cook went on to organise tours to Scotland, and later to continental Europe and beyond."),
  ('Lecturer', 'AU', "At around the same time, new guidebooks began to appear, such as those published by John Murray in Britain and Karl Baedeker in Germany, which gave practical information about routes, prices and hotels. Together, the railways, the guidebooks and the organised tour made travel for pleasure possible for millions of ordinary people. Next week, we'll look at how cheap air travel in the twentieth century transformed tourism all over again."),
 ],
 'groups': [
  G('Questions 31–40. Complete the notes below. Write ONE WORD ONLY for each answer.', [
   Q('note', 'Earlier travellers included merchants, soldiers and __________', 'pilgrims', 'and pilgrims to visit holy places', 'Pilgrims travelled to holy places.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Richard Lassels popularised the term in a 1670 guide to __________', 'Italy', 'in a guide to Italy published in 1670', 'His guide was about Italy.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'A young Grand Tourist was usually accompanied by a __________', 'tutor', 'He would usually be accompanied by a tutor', 'A tutor went with him.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Languages studied: mainly French and __________', 'Italian', 'especially French and Italian', 'French and Italian were the main languages.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'In Rome, travellers saw remains of the __________ world', 'classical', 'the remains of the classical world', 'Rome had remains of the classical world.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'At Mont Cenis, dismantled carriages were carried by __________', 'mules', 'they were taken apart and carried by mules', 'Mules carried the carriages.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Batoni painted __________ of many visitors to Rome', 'portraits', 'many visitors had their portraits painted by Pompeo Batoni', 'Batoni painted portraits.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Travel was disrupted by the French __________ and later wars', 'Revolution', 'it was badly interrupted by the French Revolution', 'The French Revolution interrupted the tour.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Cook\'s 1841 train took people to a __________ meeting', 'temperance', 'to a temperance meeting in Loughborough', 'It was a temperance meeting.', max=1),
   Q('note', 'Murray and Baedeker published practical __________', 'guidebooks', 'new guidebooks began to appear', 'They published guidebooks.', max=1),
  ]),
 ],
},
